## Bottom line

- **Not a bug** – it’s the designed “local-fonts” fallback introduced in GL JS ≥ 5.11.  
- The generic font you see simply means the browser couldn’t find the font you named.  
- Fix it by **loading the font yourself** (web-font or system) or by **adding a proper `glyphs` URL** if you need the same SDF glyphs on both GL JS and Native.

If you follow the steps above and still see the wrong typeface, double-check the console for font-loading errors and verify the exact `font-family` name. Once the font is reachable and correctly named, MapLibre will render your labels exactly as you intended.

### Short answer  
**It’s not a bug – it’s the expected “local-fonts” fallback that MapLibre GL JS uses when the style has no `glyphs` URL.**  

When `glyphs` is omitted, GL JS falls back to rendering every `text-font` entry with the browser’s normal font-stack resolution (the same algorithm CSS uses for `font-family`). If the font you named isn’t available to the browser – either because it isn’t a system font or you haven’t loaded it with `@font-face`/`document.fonts.load()` – the browser picks the first generic family it can find (usually *sans-serif*), which is why you see a “generic” look.

Below is a deeper dive into **what’s really happening**, **why you see a generic font**, and **what you can do** to get the exact font you want – or to keep using the fallback deliberately.

---

## 1. What MapLibre GL JS does when `glyphs` is missing

| Version | Behaviour when `glyphs` is omitted or a glyph PBF fails |
|---------|--------------------------------------------------------|
| **≤ 5.10** | The style is considered invalid; the map will log an error and the tile may be rendered without text. |
| **≥ 5.11** (PR #4564) | MapLibre enters **local-fonts mode**: <br>• Every `text-font` name is treated as a **CSS-style font family**. <br>• The engine asks the browser to draw the text with the resolved font (via the browser’s text rasterizer). <br>• No glyph PBFs are requested, so there is no network traffic for fonts. <br>• If the font cannot be resolved, the browser falls back to its default generic family (usually `sans-serif`). <br>• The map still works – labels are visible, just not in the exact font you named. |

> **Key point:** This fallback is **GL JS-only**. MapLibre Native still requires a valid `glyphs` URL (or a glyph server) because it has no built-in local-font rasterizer.

---

## 2. Why you see a “generic” font

1. **The font name you put in `text-font` does not match any font the browser can find.**  
   - If you wrote `"MyCustomFont Bold"` but never loaded a `@font-face` that declares that family name, the browser has nothing to match.
2. **The font is a system font that simply isn’t installed on the user’s OS.**  
   - For example, `"Noto Sans"` is not guaranteed to be present on Windows or macOS unless you ship it yourself.
3. **The font is loaded *after* the map is created** and the map never re-evaluates the style.  
   - `document.fonts.load()` is asynchronous; if the map draws before the font is ready, the first paint uses the fallback. Subsequent paints will still use the fallback unless you trigger a style refresh (`map.triggerRepaint()` or `map.setStyle(map.getStyle())`).

Because the fallback uses the **browser’s generic family**, the visual result varies by platform (Chrome on Windows → Arial, Safari on macOS → Helvetica, etc.), which is why you see a “generic” look.

---

## 3. How to get the exact font you want

### Option A – Keep the `glyphs`-less style but **load the font locally**

1. **Add a `@font-face` rule (or use a web-font service).**  
   ```css
   @font-face {
     font-family: 'MyCustomFont';
     src: url('https://example.com/fonts/MyCustomFont-Regular.woff2') format('woff2');
     font-weight: 400;
     font-style: normal;
   }
   @font-face {
     font-family: 'MyCustomFont';
     src: url('https://example.com/fonts/MyCustomFont-Bold.woff2') format('woff2');
     font-weight: 700;
     font-style: normal;
   }
   ```
2. **Make sure the `font-family` name matches exactly what you put in `text-font`.**  
   ```json
   "layout": {
     "text-field": ["get", "name"],
     "text-font": ["MyCustomFont Bold", "MyCustomFont Regular"]
   }
   ```
3. **Ensure the font is loaded before the map draws** (optional but recommended).  
   ```js
   // Wait for the font to be ready
   document.fonts.load('1rem MyCustomFont').then(() => {
     const map = new maplibregl.Map({ /* … */ });
   });
   ```
4. **(Optional) Declare the font in the style’s `font-faces` property** – this is read by both GL JS and Native and lets you limit the Unicode ranges if you want.  
   ```json
   "font-faces": {
     "MyCustomFont": [
       {
         "url": "https://example.com/fonts/MyCustomFont-Regular.ttf",
         "unicode-range": ["U+0000-00FF"]   // Latin block, for example
       }
     ]
   }
   ```

> **Result:** The map will still use the local-fonts path (no glyph PBFs), but the browser now has the exact font you asked for, so the labels appear in that font.

### Option B – Serve glyph PBFs (the classic approach)

If you prefer the original MapLibre workflow (or you need the same look on Native), add a `glyphs` URL and make sure the font-stack names match the folders on the server.

```json
{
  "glyphs": "https://mytileserver.com/fonts/{fontstack}/{range}.pbf",
  "layers": [
    {
      "id": "place-labels",
      "type": "symbol",
      "layout": {
        "text-field": ["get", "name"],
        "text-font": ["MyCustomFont Bold", "MyCustomFont Regular"]
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

- **If you already have a glyph server** (e.g., the OpenMapTiles `fonts.openmaptiles.org` endpoint), just point to it.
- **If you need custom fonts**, generate the PBFs with **Font Maker** or **fontnik**, place them under the `{fontstack}` folder hierarchy, and serve them with a static-file server that supports CORS.

> **Result:** Both GL JS and Native will request the glyph PBFs, and the exact shapes from the font file are used. No reliance on the browser’s font rasterizer.

### Option C – Hybrid: keep `glyphs` for most scripts, rely on local fallback for CJK

CJK characters are *always* rendered locally by default (`localIdeographFontFamily`), because serving glyph PBFs for every ideograph would be huge and slow. You can keep a `glyphs` URL for Latin/Arabic/Devanagari etc., and let CJK be drawn by the browser:

```js
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'style-with-glyphs.json',
  localIdeographFontFamily: '"Noto Sans CJK SC", sans-serif' // optional custom on-device CJK font
});
```

---

## 4. Checklist – Is your map behaving as intended?

| ✅ | Check |
|---|-------|
| **1** | `glyphs` is **absent** in the style → you are deliberately using the local-fonts fallback. |
| **2** | Every `text-font` entry matches a **system font** *or* a **web-font you have loaded** via `@font-face`. |
| **3** | The web-font files are reachable (CORS headers, correct MIME type). |
| **4** | The font is loaded **before** the map draws, or you trigger a style refresh after it loads. |
| **5** | If you need the same appearance on Native, **add a `glyphs` URL** (Native cannot use the local-fonts fallback). |
| **6** | For CJK, you can leave `localIdeographFontFamily` at its default (`'sans-serif'`) or set it to a nicer on-device CJK font. |
| **7** | For Arabic/Hebrew/RTL scripts, you are on GL JS ≥ 6.9.0 (no RTL plugin needed). |

If all of the above are true and you still see a generic font, double-check the **exact family name** (case-sensitive, spaces, etc.) and that the font file actually contains the glyphs you need.

---

## 5. TL;DR – What to do next

1. **If you want the font you named:**  
   - Load it as a web font (`@font-face`) **or** install it on the client OS.  
   - Ensure the `font-family` name matches the `text-font` strings.  
   - Optionally declare it in `font-faces` for consistency across GL JS and Native.

2. **If you want the same look on Native as well:**  
   - Add a `glyphs` URL that serves the font’s PBFs.  
   - Keep the `font-faces` entry (Native reads it for shaping).  

3. **If you’re fine with the generic look:**  
   - Nothing to fix – you’re already in the intended local-fonts mode.  

---

### Quick code example (local-font mode)

```html
<link rel="stylesheet" href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@latest/dist/maplibre-gl.css" />
<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@latest/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>

<style>
@font-face {
  font-family: 'MyCustomFont';
  src: url('https://example.com/fonts/MyCustomFont-Regular.woff2') format('woff2');
}
</style>

<div id="map" style="width:100%;height:100vh;"></div>

<script>
document.fonts.load('1rem MyCustomFont').then(() => {
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: { osm: { type: 'raster', tiles: ['https://a.tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png'], tileSize: 256 } },
      layers: [
        {
          id: 'water',
          type: 'background',
          paint: { 'background-color': '#e0f7fa' }
        },
        {
          id: 'place-labels',
          type: 'symbol',
          source: 'osm',
          layout: {
            'text-field': ['get', 'name'],
            'text-font': ['MyCustomFont Bold', 'MyCustomFont Regular'],
            'text-size': 14
          },
          paint: { 'text-color': '#111' }
        }
      ]
      // No `glyphs` property → local-fonts mode
    }
  });
});
</script>
```

Now the labels will appear in **MyCustomFont** (provided the font file loads successfully). If you remove the `@font-face` rule, the same map will fall back to the generic sans-serif, exactly what you observed.

---

### Bottom line

- **No `glyphs` → local-fonts fallback** (expected behavior).  
- Generic font = the browser couldn’t find the font you named.  
- Fix it by **loading the font** (or by adding a proper `glyphs` URL if you need the classic glyph-PBF path, especially for Native).  

That’s all there is to it—no hidden bug, just the new default rendering path introduced in MapLibre GL JS 5.11. Happy styling!

## Short answer  

**Yes – you can copy the same `font-faces` block into a MapLibre GL JS style and you will get the same “offline-font” behaviour, provided you also make the font files reachable to the browser (or cache them with a Service Worker).**  

The only things that change between Native and GL JS are:

| What you need to check | Native | GL JS |
|------------------------|--------|-------|
| **`font-faces` property** | read automatically (Android 11.13+, iOS 6.18+) | read automatically **if the style contains it** (since GL JS 6.7.0). You can also call `map.setFontFaces()` at runtime to add or replace entries. |
| **How the font files are fetched** | `url` can be a file-system path (`file:///…`) or an HTTP URL that the native app can reach. | `url` **must be an HTTP(S) URL that the browser can fetch** (same-origin or CORS-enabled). A `file://` URL will not work in the browser. |
| **Offline availability** | You ship the `.ttf/.otf` files with the app bundle. | You must either (a) host the files on a server that the page can reach **and** cache them (e.g., Service Worker, `Cache` API, or `preload`), **or** (b) embed them as data-URIs / base-64 strings in the style. |
| **Fallback when a glyph is missing** | Uses the local-font path if the glyph PBF cannot be loaded. | Same fallback exists **only when `glyphs` is omitted or a request fails** (GL JS ≥ 5.11). If you keep a `glyphs` URL, the engine will still try to load the PBF first, then fall back to the local font for the missing range. |

Below is a step-by-step guide that shows how to move the exact same `font-faces` block from your Native stylesheet into a GL JS style, what you have to adjust, and a few optional tricks to make the whole thing truly offline.

---

## 1. Keep the `font-faces` block exactly as-is (except for URL format)

If your Native style looks like this:

```json
{
  "version": 8,
  "glyphs": "https://example.com/fonts/{fontstack}/{range}.pbf",
  "font-faces": {
    "MyBrand Regular": [
      {
        "url": "file:///android_asset/fonts/MyBrand-Regular.ttf",
        "unicode-range": ["U+0000-00FF", "U+0100-024F"]
      }
    ],
    "MyBrand Bold": [
      {
        "url": "file:///android_asset/fonts/MyBrand-Bold.ttf",
        "unicode-range": ["U+0000-00FF", "U+0100-024F"]
      }
    ]
  },
  "layers": [ … ]
}
```

You can copy the `font-faces` object **verbatim** into the GL JS style, but you must replace the `file://…` URLs with something the browser can request, e.g.:

```json
{
  "version": 8,
  "glyphs": "https://example.com/fonts/{fontstack}/{range}.pbf",
  "font-faces": {
    "MyBrand Regular": [
      {
        "url": "https://mycdn.example.com/fonts/MyBrand-Regular.ttf",
        "unicode-range": ["U+0000-00FF", "U+0100-024F"]
      }
    ],
    "MyBrand Bold": [
      {
        "url": "https://mycdn.example.com/fonts/MyBrand-Bold.ttf",
        "unicode-range": ["U+0000-00FF", "U+0100-024F"]
      }
    ]
  },
  "layers": [ … ]
}
```

> **Why the change?**  
> In a browser the only protocols that can be used for `url` are `http:`/`https:` (or `data:`). Anything that points to a local file system is blocked by the same-origin policy.

If you cannot host the fonts on a remote server, you can embed them directly:

```json
{
  "font-faces": {
    "MyBrand Regular": [
      {
        "url": "data:font/ttf;base64,AAEAAAARAQAABAA…",   // base64-encoded TTF
        "unicode-range": ["U+0000-00FF"]
      }
    ]
  }
}
```

Embedding works for small fonts, but the style file can become huge, so for anything larger than a few kilobytes a CDN or static-file host is recommended.

---

## 2. Make sure the browser can actually fetch the files

### 2.1 CORS

If the fonts are served from a different origin than your web page, the server **must** send the appropriate CORS headers:

```
Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *
```

or, more securely, the exact origin of your page.

If you forget this, the browser will block the request and you’ll see an error like:

```
Access to font at 'https://mycdn.example.com/fonts/MyBrand-Regular.ttf' from origin 'https://myapp.com' has been blocked by CORS policy
```

### 2.2 MIME type

Serve the files with the correct `Content-Type`:

* `font/ttf` for `.ttf`
* `font/otf` for `.otf`
* `font/woff` / `font/woff2` for the compressed variants (recommended for the web)

Most CDNs set these automatically; if you’re using a custom static server, add them to the response headers.

---

## 3. Offline-first strategy (so the app truly works without a network)

### 3.1 Service Worker + Cache API (recommended)

```js
self.addEventListener('install', event => {
  event.waitUntil(
    caches.open('maplibre-fonts').then(cache => {
      return cache.addAll([
        'https://mycdn.example.com/fonts/MyBrand-Regular.ttf',
        'https://mycdn.example.com/fonts/MyBrand-Bold.ttf'
      ]);
    })
  );
});

self.addEventListener('fetch', event => {
  const url = new URL(event.request.url);
  if (url.pathname.startsWith('/fonts/')) {
    // Try the network first, fall back to cache
    event.respondWith(
      fetch(event.request).catch(() => caches.match(event.request))
    );
    return;
  }
});
```

* The Service Worker caches the font files during the first install.  
* When the user goes offline, the fetch handler serves the cached fonts, and MapLibre GL JS will still be able to resolve `font-faces`.

### 3.2 Pre-load the fonts with `<link rel="preload">`

```html
<link rel="preload" href="https://mycdn.example.com/fonts/MyBrand-Regular.ttf"
      as="font" type="font/ttf" crossorigin>
<link rel="preload" href="https://mycdn.example.com/fonts/MyBrand-Bold.ttf"
      as="font" type="font/ttf" crossorigin>
```

Preloading reduces the time between map creation and the first label draw, but it does **not** make the fonts available offline unless you also cache them (e.g., via a Service Worker).

### 3.3 Bundling with the app (data-URI approach)

If you truly want a *single* HTML file that works offline (e.g., a static demo), embed the fonts as data-URIs in the `font-faces` block as shown earlier. The browser will have the font data immediately, no network request is needed.

---

## 4. How GL JS actually uses `font-faces`

1. **During style parsing** it builds a map from `text-font` names → list of font files (and their Unicode ranges).  
2. **When a label is drawn** it looks up the first `text-font` entry that covers the code point(s) of the label.  
   * If the code point falls inside a `unicode-range` of a declared file, that file is fetched (or taken from the cache) and used.  
   * If the code point is **not** covered, the engine falls back to the `glyphs` URL (if present) or to the GL JS local-font fallback (TinySDF) if the glyph PBF fails or `glyphs` is omitted.  
3. **Shaping** (Arabic, Devanagari, etc.) is performed by the browser’s text engine **as long as a `font-faces` entry exists for the script**. The glyph PBFs for those scripts are ignored after GL JS 6.15.0, so you can safely keep a `glyphs` URL for other scripts (Latin, CJK, etc.) without harming the shaping.

> **Tip:** If you want to guarantee that *all* scripts are drawn from your own files (including CJK), set `localIdeographFontFamily: false` **and** provide a `font-faces` entry for the CJK font. This will make GL JS request the CJK glyph PBFs, which can be a lot of traffic, so only do it if you really need the exact glyph shapes.

---

## 5. Example: Full GL JS style with offline fonts

```json
{
  "version": 8,
  "glyphs": "https://mycdn.example.com/font/{fontstack}/{range}.pbf",
  "font-faces": {
    "MyBrand Regular": [
      {
        "url": "https://mycdn.example.com/fonts/MyBrand-Regular.ttf",
        "unicode-range": ["U+0000-00FF", "U+0100-024F"]
      }
    ],
    "MyBrand Bold": [
      {
        "url": "https://mycdn.example.com/fonts/MyBrand-Bold.ttf",
        "unicode-range": ["U+0000-00FF", "U+0100-024F"]
      }
    ],
    "Noto Sans CJK SC": [
      {
        "url": "https://mycdn.example.com/fonts/NotoSansCJKsc-Regular.otf",
        "unicode-range": ["U+4E00-9FFF", "U+3400-4DBF"]
      }
    ]
  },
  "layers": [
    {
      "id": "place-labels",
      "type": "symbol",
      "source": "osm",
      "source-layer": "place",
      "layout": {
        "text-field": ["get", "name"],
        "text-font": ["MyBrand Bold", "Noto Sans CJK SC", "Noto Sans Regular"],
        "text-size": 14
      },
      "paint": {
        "text-color": "#222"
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

**What this does**

* Latin/Greek/Cyrillic characters → `MyBrand Bold` (served from the TTF you bundled).  
* CJK characters → `Noto Sans CJK SC` (served from the OTF you bundled).  
* Any code point not covered by the two entries → falls back to the `glyphs` URL (or to TinySDF if the request fails).  

If you want *purely offline* operation, you can **remove** the `glyphs` field entirely. GL JS will then use the local-font fallback for everything that isn’t covered by a `font-faces` entry (e.g., emoji). The map will still render, but you lose the SDF scaling for those uncovered characters.

---

## 6. Common pitfalls & how to avoid them

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| Labels appear **blank** or with the default “MapLibre” font | `font-faces` URLs 404 or blocked by CORS | Verify the URLs in the network tab, add `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` header, or host the fonts on the same origin. |
| Text shows **different fonts** on different browsers | No `unicode-range` or overlapping ranges, or the browser picks a different fallback | Keep `unicode-range` tight and non-overlapping. Test on Chrome, Firefox, Safari. |
| After going **offline** the map still works, but labels turn into **boxes** | Fonts were not cached (no Service Worker) | Add a Service Worker that caches the font URLs, or embed them as data-URIs. |
| **CJK** characters look huge or mis-aligned | `localIdeographFontFamily` set to `false` and you didn’t provide a CJK `font-faces` entry | Either keep the default (`true`) or add a proper CJK entry and keep `localIdeographFontFamily` false. |
| **Arabic** text is rendered left-to-right | Using GL JS < 6.9.0 without the RTL plugin | Upgrade to ≥ 6.9.0 or load `setRTLTextPlugin` before creating the map. |
| Font weight (e.g., `Bold`) is ignored | You only listed the regular TTF in `font-faces` but used `"MyBrand Bold"` in

## TL;DR  
Yes – the **`font-faces`** block you use in a MapLibre Native style can be copied verbatim into a MapLibre GL JS style and it will work the same way **as long as** the font files are reachable by the browser (i.e. served over HTTP(S) from the same origin or a CORS-enabled origin) and you either:

1. **Keep a `glyphs` URL** (so GL JS can still request glyph PBFs for scripts that are not drawn locally) **or**  
2. **Omit the `glyphs` field** (or set it to an empty string) so GL JS falls back to the local-font path for *all* scripts.

The rest of the behaviour (Unicode-range filtering, HarfBuzz shaping, right-to-left handling, etc.) is identical to the native SDK.

Below is a step-by-step guide, the things you must watch out for, and a minimal working example.

---

## 1. What `font-faces` does in GL JS

| Feature | Native | GL JS (≥ 6.7.0) |
|---------|--------|----------------|
| Reads the `font-faces` property from the style JSON | ✅ (Android 11.13+, iOS 6.18+) | ✅ (since 6.7.0) |
| Uses the `unicode-range` entries to decide which file supplies which code-points | ✅ | ✅ |
| Shapes complex scripts (Arabic, Devanagari, Thai, etc.) with HarfBuzz | ✅ | ✅ |
| Works when `glyphs` is **omitted** (pure local-font mode) | ❌ (no fallback) | ✅ (since 5.11.0) |
| Works when a glyph PBF fails to load (fallback to local fonts) | ❌ | ✅ |
| Requires the font files to be reachable via a **URL** that the browser can fetch | N/A (can be a file path on-device) | ✅ (must be HTTP(S) or a data-URL) |

So the **only practical difference** is the way the files are fetched.

---

## 2. Copying the `font-faces` block

### 2.1. Keep the JSON exactly as you have it

```json
{
  "version": 8,
  "glyphs": "https://my-tileserver.com/fonts/{fontstack}/{range}.pbf",
  "font-faces": {
    "Noto Sans Regular": [
      {
        "url": "https://example.com/fonts/NotoSans-Regular.ttf",
        "unicode-range": ["U+0000-00FF", "U+0100-024F"]
      }
    ],
    "Noto Sans Devanagari": [
      {
        "url": "https://example.com/fonts/NotoSansDevanagari-Regular.ttf",
        "unicode-range": ["U+0900-097F"]
      }
    ],
    "Noto Sans Arabic": [
      {
        "url": "https://example.com/fonts/NotoSansArabic-Regular.ttf",
        "unicode-range": ["U+0600-06FF"]
      }
    ]
  },
  "layers": [ … ]
}
```

*No changes are required* – GL JS will read the block the same way native does.

### 2.2. Make sure the URLs are **browser-friendly**

| ✅ Good | ❌ Bad |
|--------|-------|
| `https://example.com/fonts/NotoSans-Regular.ttf` (served with `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` or your site’s origin) | `file:///android_asset/fonts/NotoSans-Regular.ttf` |
| `data:font/ttf;base64,…` (inline data URL) | Relative path that points to a location the web server does not expose |

If you are bundling the app with a static site generator, you can place the `.ttf/.otf` files in your `public/` folder and reference them with a relative URL (`"./fonts/NotoSans-Regular.ttf"`). Just remember that the **same origin** rule applies – the fonts must be served from the same domain (or a CORS-enabled domain).

### 2.3. Optional: set the fonts at runtime

If you need to add or replace a `font-face` after the map has been created (e.g. after a Service Worker finishes caching), call:

```js
map.setFontFaces({
  "Noto Sans Regular": [
    { url: "/fonts/NotoSans-Regular.ttf", unicodeRange: ["U+0000-00FF"] }
  ],
  // …other faces
});
```

`setFontFaces` merges with the style’s existing `font-faces` block, so you can keep the JSON static and only augment it when needed.

---

## 3. Offline-first considerations

### 3.1. First-time fetch is still a network request

Even though the fonts are *local* to the device after they have been downloaded, the browser **must** fetch them once. To make the app truly offline you have two options:

| Approach | How it works |
|----------|--------------|
| **Pre-bundle the fonts** (include them in the build) | The browser loads them from the same origin as the HTML/JS, no network request needed after the initial page load. |
| **Cache them with a Service Worker** | The Service Worker intercepts the `GET` for the `.ttf/.otf` files, stores them in the Cache API, and serves them on subsequent loads even when the network is unavailable. |

Both approaches are compatible with `font-faces`. The only extra step is to make sure the URLs you put in `font-faces` match the URLs the Service Worker caches.

### 3.2. CJK (Chinese/Japanese/Korean) – special handling

*GL JS* treats CJK characters **locally** by default via the `localIdeographFontFamily` option. This means the `text-font` you list for CJK is **ignored** (except for the weight keyword). If you want to use a specific on-device CJK font you can set:

```js
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: "map",
  style: "style.json",
  localIdeographFontFamily: '"Noto Sans CJK SC", "Noto Sans CJK JP", sans-serif'
});
```

If you *do* want to serve CJK glyph PBFs (e.g. for a brand-specific CJK font), set `localIdeographFontFamily: false`. That will make GL JS request the glyph ranges just like any other script, and the `font-faces` block will be consulted for shaping.

### 3.3. Glyphs URL – keep it or drop it?

| Scenario | Recommendation |
|----------|----------------|
| **You have a working glyph server** (e.g. `https://my-tileserver.com/font/{fontstack}/{range}.pbf`) and you only need the `font-faces` for complex scripts (Arabic, Devanagari, etc.) | **Leave the `glyphs` field**. GL JS will still request glyph PBFs for Latin, Cyrillic, etc., but for the scripts covered by `font-faces` the browser-provided font will be used. |
| **You want a completely offline map** (no network at all) | **Remove the `glyphs` field** (or set it to an empty string). GL JS will fall back to the local-font path for *all* scripts, including Latin. This works because GL JS ≥ 5.11.0 treats a missing `glyphs` URL as “use local fonts”. |

> **Important:** Native does **not** support a missing `glyphs` field, so if you need the *same* style to work on both platforms you must keep the field and accept that Native will always try to fetch glyph PBFs. The GL JS fallback is a GL-only convenience.

---

## 4. Full minimal example (offline-first)

```html
<!doctype html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <title>MapLibre GL JS – offline font-faces</title>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@6.15.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>#map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }</style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script type="module">
import maplibregl from 'https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@6.15.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js';

// ---- Service Worker registration (optional) ----
if ('serviceWorker' in navigator) {
  navigator.serviceWorker.register('/sw.js');
}

// ---- Map creation ----
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: {
    "version": 8,
    "glyphs": "https://my-tileserver.com/font/{fontstack}/{range}.pbf", // keep if you have a server
    // Omit the line above if you want *pure* offline mode
    "font-faces": {
      "Noto Sans Regular": [
        {
          "url": "/fonts/NotoSans-Regular.ttf",
          "unicode-range": ["U+0000-00FF", "U+0100-024F"]
        }
      ],
      "Noto Sans Devanagari": [
        {
          "url": "/fonts/NotoSansDevanagari-Regular.ttf",
          "unicode-range": ["U+0900-097F"]
        }
      ],
      "Noto Sans Arabic": [
        {
          "url": "/fonts/NotoSansArabic-Regular.ttf",
          "unicode-range": ["U+0600-06FF"]
        }
      ]
    },
    "sources": {
      "osm": {
        "type": "vector",
        "url": "https://tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf"
      }
    },
    "layers": [
      {
        "id": "place-labels",
        "type": "symbol",
        "source": "osm",
        "source-layer": "place",
        "layout": {
          "text-field": ["coalesce", ["get", "name:en"], ["get", "name"]],
          "text-font": ["Noto Sans Regular", "Noto Sans Devanagari", "Noto Sans Arabic"],
          "text-size": 14
        },
        "paint": {
          "text-color": "#222"
        }
      }
    ]
  },
  center: [77.2, 28.6],
  zoom: 5,
  // optional: change CJK local font
  // localIdeographFontFamily: '"Noto Sans CJK SC", sans-serif'
});
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

**`/sw.js` (very simple cache-first worker)**

```js
self.addEventListener('install', e => e.waitUntil(self.skipWaiting()));
self.addEventListener('activate', e => e.waitUntil(self.clients.claim()));

self.addEventListener('fetch', event => {
  const url = new URL(event.request.url);
  // Cache only the font files (you can add sprites, style JSON, etc.)
  if (url.pathname.endsWith('.ttf') || url.pathname.endsWith('.otf')) {
    event.respondWith(
      caches.open('font-cache').then(cache =>
        cache.match(event.request).then(resp => {
          return resp || fetch(event.request).then(networkResp => {
            cache.put(event.request, networkResp.clone());
            return networkResp;
          });
        })
      )
    );
  }
});
```

*Result*: The map loads, the label layer uses the three local fonts for Latin, Devanagari, and Arabic, and after the first load the fonts are served from the Service Worker cache, so the app works offline.

---

## 5. Common pitfalls & how to avoid them

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| **Labels disappear** (or become “tofu”) for a script you added to `font-faces` | The `unicode-range` does **not** include the code-points you are trying to render, or the URL is 404 / blocked by CORS. | Verify the range with a Unicode chart, open the URL directly in the browser, and

## TL;DR

| What you need | How to do it |
|---------------|--------------|
| **Arabic / Hebrew shaping & RTL layout** | • If you are on **MapLibre GL JS ≥ 6.9.0** – nothing extra, the engine does it automatically. <br>• If you are on **MapLibre GL JS < 6.9.0** (e.g. 6.0-6.8) – load the **RTL-text plugin** *once* **before** you create the map. |
| **A font that contains the script** | Add the appropriate Noto (or other) font to the style’s `font-faces` (or use a glyph-server that serves the Noto-Arabic / Noto-Hebrew PBFs). |
| **Correct `text-font` fallback list** | Put the script-specific font name first (e.g. `"Noto Sans Arabic Regular"`), then a generic fallback (`"Noto Sans Regular"`). |
| **(Optional) `localIdeographFontFamily`** | Leave the default (`'sans-serif'`). It only affects CJK, not Arabic/Hebrew. |

Below is a step-by-step guide that works for **any MapLibre GL JS 6.x** map, with notes for the two version ranges.

---

## 1. Know which MapLibre version you are really using

```js
console.log(maplibregl.version);   // e.g. "6.8.2"
```

- **≥ 6.9.0** – the library already contains a built-in HarfBuzz-based shaping engine. No plugin is required.
- **< 6.9.0** – you must load the **RTL-text plugin** (`@mapbox/mapbox-gl-rtl-text`) **before** `new maplibregl.Map()`.

> **Why the split?**  
> Before 6.9.0 the core renderer only knew how to draw left-to-right glyphs. The plugin supplies the Unicode-Bidi algorithm, Arabic joining, and Hebrew mirroring. Starting with 6.9.0 the same code lives inside the core, and the plugin is deprecated.

---

## 2. (If needed) Load the RTL-text plugin

```html
<!-- 1️⃣ Include the plugin script (any version ≥ 0.3.0 works) -->
<script src="https://unpkg.com/@mapbox/mapbox-gl-rtl-text@0.3.0/dist/mapbox-gl-rtl-text.js"></script>

<script>
  // 2️⃣ Tell MapLibre to use it – the second argument (`false`) means “do not
  //    block map creation while the script loads”.  You can also pass `true`
  //    if you prefer a synchronous load.
  maplibregl.setRTLTextPlugin(
    // URL of the plugin (must be reachable from the page)
    'https://unpkg.com/@mapbox/mapbox-gl-rtl-text@0.3.0/dist/mapbox-gl-rtl-text.js',
    // Callback – optional, useful for debugging
    function (error) {
      if (error) {
        console.error('RTL plugin failed to load:', error);
      } else {
        console.log('RTL plugin loaded successfully');
      }
    },
    // `false` = async load (recommended)
    false
  );
</script>
```

> **Important:** The call to `setRTLTextPlugin` **must happen before** you instantiate the map. If you call it after `new maplibregl.Map()`, the map will render the first frame without shaping.

---

## 3. Provide a font that actually contains Arabic / Hebrew glyphs

### 3-a. Using a **glyph-server** (the classic way)

If your style already has a `glyphs` URL that points at a server (e.g. `https://demotiles.maplibre.org/font/{fontstack}/{range}.pbf`), make sure the server includes the Noto Arabic / Hebrew stacks:

```json
{
  "glyphs": "https://my-fonts.example.com/font/{fontstack}/{range}.pbf",
  "layers": [
    {
      "id": "place-labels",
      "type": "symbol",
      "source": "osm",
      "source-layer": "place",
      "layout": {
        "text-field": ["get", "name"],
        "text-font": [
          "Noto Sans Arabic Regular",   // Arabic-specific
          "Noto Sans Hebrew Regular",   // Hebrew-specific
          "Noto Sans Regular"           // generic fallback
        ],
        "text-size": 12
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

The font-stack names **must match the folder names** on the glyph server (`Noto%20Sans%20Arabic%20Regular`, etc.). If you are using the public demo server (`demotiles.maplibre.org`), those stacks are already present.

### 3-b. Using **`font-faces`** (recommended for GL JS ≥ 6.7)

Starting with GL JS 6.7 you can ship **web-font files** directly in the style. This works for both GL JS and MapLibre Native (Android 11.13+, iOS 6.18+).

```json
{
  "font-faces": {
    "Noto Sans Arabic Regular": [
      {
        "url": "https://example.com/fonts/NotoSansArabic-Regular.ttf",
        "unicode-range": ["U+0600-06FF", "U+0750-077F", "U+08A0-08FF", "U+FB50-FDFF", "U+FE70-FEFF", "U+1EE00-1EEFF"]
      }
    ],
    "Noto Sans Hebrew Regular": [
      {
        "url": "https://example.com/fonts/NotoSansHebrew-Regular.ttf",
        "unicode-range": ["U+0590-05FF", "U+FB1D-FB4F"]
      }
    ]
  },

  "glyphs": "https://my-fonts.example.com/font/{fontstack}/{range}.pbf",   // can stay, it will be ignored for the ranges covered by font-faces
  ...
}
```

**Why use `font-faces`?**

* The browser (or Native’s HarfBuzz) does the shaping, so you get proper Arabic joining and Hebrew mirroring even if the glyph PBFs are missing for those code points.
* You can host the TTF/OTF files on the same origin as your app (or on a CDN) – no need for a separate glyph-server for those scripts.
* The same `font-faces` block is read by MapLibre Native, guaranteeing visual parity across platforms.

> **Tip:** If you already have a glyph server that serves Noto Arabic/Hebrew, you can skip `font-faces`. The fallback will still work, but `font-faces` gives you tighter control over which exact TTF is used (e.g., you might want a brand-specific Arabic font).

---

## 4. Set the `text-font` property correctly

`text-font` is a **fallback list**. The first entry that the renderer can resolve (via glyphs **or** `font-faces`) wins.

```json
"text-font": [
  "Noto Sans Arabic Regular",   // Arabic-specific (covers U+0600-U+06FF etc.)
  "Noto Sans Hebrew Regular",   // Hebrew-specific (covers U+0590-U+05FF)
  "Noto Sans Regular"           // generic Latin fallback
]
```

*If you have separate layers for Arabic-only and Hebrew-only labels, you can shorten the list, but a single universal list works fine.*

---

## 5. (Optional) Verify that the map is actually shaping the text

### 5-a. Quick visual test

Add a label that contains a known Arabic ligature, e.g. **"العربية"** (Arabic) or a Hebrew word **"ישראל"** (Israel). If the letters appear joined (Arabic) or right-to-left (Hebrew) you’re good.

### 5-b. Console check (GL JS ≥ 6.7)

```js
map.on('styledata', () => {
  const label = map.queryRenderedFeatures({ layers: ['place-labels'] })[0];
  console.log('Rendered text:', label?.properties?.name);
});
```

If the string you supplied is exactly what you see in the console, the shaping succeeded.

### 5-c. Debugging common pitfalls

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| Text appears **left-to-right** (Hebrew/Arabic letters reversed) | RTL plugin not loaded **or** loaded *after* map creation. | Move `setRTLTextPlugin` call before `new maplibregl.Map()`. |
| Arabic letters are **disconnected** (each character isolated) | Using a glyph server **without** Arabic joining support **and** no `font-faces`. | Add `font-faces` with a TTF that contains Arabic shaping, or upgrade to GL JS ≥ 6.9.0 (no plugin needed). |
| Some characters are missing (boxes) | Font file does not cover the Unicode range, or `unicode-range` in `font-faces` is too narrow. | Extend the `unicode-range` or use a more complete font (e.g., Noto Sans Arabic). |
| The map throws `Unable to load glyph range …` errors | `glyphs` URL points at a server that lacks the requested range, or the font-stack name is miss-spelled. | Verify the folder names on the server match the `text-font` strings exactly. |
| Labels flicker on first load, then look correct | Plugin loaded **asynchronously** but the map rendered a frame before it was ready. | Pass `true` as the third argument to `setRTLTextPlugin` (synchronous load) **or** call `map.once('load', …)` after the plugin’s callback. |

---

## 6. Full minimal example (GL JS 6.8 – needs plugin)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>Arabic & Hebrew RTL labels</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@6.8.2/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@6.8.2/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <!-- 1️⃣ RTL plugin -->
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/@mapbox/mapbox-gl-rtl-text@0.3.0/dist/mapbox-gl-rtl-text.js"></script>
  <script>
    // 2️⃣ Register the plugin *before* creating the map
    maplibregl.setRTLTextPlugin(
      'https://unpkg.com/@mapbox/mapbox-gl-rtl-text@0.3.0/dist/mapbox-gl-rtl-text.js',
      function (err) {
        if (err) console.error('RTL plugin error', err);
        else console.log('RTL plugin ready');
      },
      false // async
    );
  </script>
  <style>
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      "version": 8,
      "glyphs": "https://demotiles.maplibre.org/font/{fontstack}/{range}.pbf",
      "sources": {
        "osm": {
          "type": "vector",
          "url": "https://demotiles.maplibre.org/tiles/planet.json"
        }
      },
      "layers": [
        {
          "id": "place-labels",
          "type": "symbol",
          "source": "osm",
          "source-layer": "place",
          "layout": {
            "text-field": ["get", "name"],
            "text-font": [
              "Noto Sans Arabic Regular",
              "Noto Sans Hebrew Regular",
              "Noto Sans Regular"
            ],
            "text-size": 14,
            "text-anchor": "center"
          },
          "paint": {
            "text-color": "#222"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    center: [35, 31], // somewhere in the Middle East
    zoom: 5
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

*If you upgrade to **MapLibre GL JS 6.9.0** or newer, simply delete the `<script>` that loads the RTL plugin and the `setRTLTextPlugin` call

## TL;DR

| What you need | How to do it |
|---------------|--------------|
| **Arabic / Hebrew shaping & RTL layout** | • If you are on **MapLibre GL JS ≥ 6.9.0** – nothing extra, the engine does it automatically. <br>• If you are on **MapLibre GL JS < 6.9.0** (e.g. 6.0-6.8) – load the **RTL-text plugin** *once* **before** you create the map. |
| **A font that contains the script** | Add the appropriate Noto (or other) font to the style’s `font-faces` (or use a glyph-server that serves the Noto-Arabic / Noto-Hebrew PBFs). |
| **Correct `text-font` fallback list** | Put the script-specific font name first (e.g. `"Noto Sans Arabic Regular"`), then a generic fallback (`"Noto Sans Regular"`). |
| **(Optional) `localIdeographFontFamily`** | Leave the default (`'sans-serif'`). It only affects CJK, not Arabic/Hebrew. |

Below is a step-by-step guide that works for **any MapLibre GL JS 6.x** map, with notes for the two version ranges.

---

## 1. Know which MapLibre version you are really using

```js
console.log(maplibregl.version);   // e.g. "6.8.2"
```

- **≥ 6.9.0** – the library already contains a built-in HarfBuzz-based shaping engine. No plugin is required.
- **< 6.9.0** – you must load the **RTL-text plugin** (`@mapbox/mapbox-gl-rtl-text`) **before** `new maplibregl.Map()`.

> **Why the split?**  
> Before 6.9.0 the core renderer only knew how to draw left-to-right glyphs. The plugin supplies the Unicode-Bidi algorithm, Arabic joining, and Hebrew mirroring. Starting with 6.9.0 the same code lives inside the core, and the plugin is deprecated.

---

## 2. (If needed) Load the RTL-text plugin

```html
<!-- 1️⃣ Include the plugin script (any version ≥ 0.3.0 works) -->
<script src="https://unpkg.com/@mapbox/mapbox-gl-rtl-text@0.3.0/dist/mapbox-gl-rtl-text.js"></script>

<script>
  // 2️⃣ Tell MapLibre to use it – the second argument (`false`) means “do not
  //    block map creation while the script loads”.  You can also pass `true`
  //    if you prefer a synchronous load.
  maplibregl.setRTLTextPlugin(
    // URL of the plugin (must be reachable from the page)
    'https://unpkg.com/@mapbox/mapbox-gl-rtl-text@0.3.0/dist/mapbox-gl-rtl-text.js',
    // Callback – optional, useful for debugging
    function (error) {
      if (error) {
        console.error('RTL plugin failed to load:', error);
      } else {
        console.log('RTL plugin loaded successfully');
      }
    },
    // `false` = async load (recommended)
    false
  );
</script>
```

> **Important:** The call to `setRTLTextPlugin` **must happen before** you instantiate the map. If you call it after `new maplibregl.Map()`, the map will render the first frame without shaping.

---

## 3. Provide a font that actually contains Arabic / Hebrew glyphs

### 3-a. Using a **glyph-server** (the classic way)

If your style already has a `glyphs` URL that points at a server (e.g. `https://demotiles.maplibre.org/font/{fontstack}/{range}.pbf`), make sure the server includes the Noto Arabic / Hebrew stacks:

```json
{
  "glyphs": "https://my-fonts.example.com/font/{fontstack}/{range}.pbf",
  "layers": [
    {
      "id": "place-labels",
      "type": "symbol",
      "source": "osm",
      "source-layer": "place",
      "layout": {
        "text-field": ["get", "name"],
        "text-font": [
          "Noto Sans Arabic Regular",   // Arabic-specific
          "Noto Sans Hebrew Regular",   // Hebrew-specific
          "Noto Sans Regular"           // generic fallback
        ],
        "text-size": 12
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

The font-stack names **must match the folder names** on the glyph server (`Noto%20Sans%20Arabic%20Regular`, etc.). If you are using the public demo server (`demotiles.maplibre.org`), those stacks are already present.

### 3-b. Using **`font-faces`** (recommended for GL JS ≥ 6.7)

Starting with GL JS 6.7 you can ship **web-font files** directly in the style. This works for both GL JS and MapLibre Native (Android 11.13+, iOS 6.18+).

```json
{
  "font-faces": {
    "Noto Sans Arabic Regular": [
      {
        "url": "https://example.com/fonts/NotoSansArabic-Regular.ttf",
        "unicode-range": ["U+0600-06FF", "U+0750-077F", "U+08A0-08FF", "U+FB50-FDFF", "U+FE70-FEFF", "U+1EE00-1EEFF"]
      }
    ],
    "Noto Sans Hebrew Regular": [
      {
        "url": "https://example.com/fonts/NotoSansHebrew-Regular.ttf",
        "unicode-range": ["U+0590-05FF", "U+FB1D-FB4F"]
      }
    ]
  },

  "glyphs": "https://my-fonts.example.com/font/{fontstack}/{range}.pbf",   // can stay, it will be ignored for the ranges covered by font-faces
  ...
}
```

**Why use `font-faces`?**

* The browser (or Native’s HarfBuzz) does the shaping, so you get proper Arabic joining and Hebrew mirroring even if the glyph PBFs are missing for those code points.
* You can host the TTF/OTF files on the same origin as your app (or on a CDN) – no need for a separate glyph-server for those scripts.
* The same `font-faces` block is read by MapLibre Native, guaranteeing visual parity across platforms.

> **Tip:** If you already have a glyph server that serves Noto Arabic/Hebrew, you can skip `font-faces`. The fallback will still work, but `font-faces` gives you tighter control over which exact TTF is used (e.g., you might want a brand-specific Arabic font).

---

## 4. Set the `text-font` property correctly

`text-font` is a **fallback list**. The first entry that the renderer can resolve (via glyphs **or** `font-faces`) wins.

```json
"text-font": [
  "Noto Sans Arabic Regular",   // Arabic-specific (covers U+0600-U+06FF etc.)
  "Noto Sans Hebrew Regular",   // Hebrew-specific (covers U+0590-U+05FF)
  "Noto Sans Regular"           // generic Latin fallback
]
```

*If you have separate layers for Arabic-only and Hebrew-only labels, you can shorten the list, but a single universal list works fine.*

---

## 5. (Optional) Verify that the map is actually shaping the text

### 5-a. Quick visual test

Add a label that contains a known Arabic ligature, e.g. **"العربية"** (Arabic) or a Hebrew word **"ישראל"** (Israel). If the letters appear joined (Arabic) or right-to-left (Hebrew) you’re good.

### 5-b. Console check (GL JS ≥ 6.7)

```js
map.on('styledata', () => {
  const label = map.queryRenderedFeatures({ layers: ['place-labels'] })[0];
  console.log('Rendered text:', label?.properties?.name);
});
```

If the string you supplied is exactly what you see in the console, the shaping succeeded.

### 5-c. Debugging common pitfalls

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| Text appears **left-to-right** (Hebrew/Arabic letters reversed) | RTL plugin not loaded **or** loaded *after* map creation. | Move `setRTLTextPlugin` call before `new maplibregl.Map()`. |
| Arabic letters are **disconnected** (each character isolated) | Using a glyph server **without** Arabic joining support **and** no `font-faces`. | Add `font-faces` with a TTF that contains Arabic shaping, or upgrade to GL JS ≥ 6.9.0 (no plugin needed). |
| Some characters are missing (boxes) | Font file does not cover the Unicode range, or `unicode-range` in `font-faces` is too narrow. | Extend the `unicode-range` or use a more complete font (e.g., Noto Sans Arabic). |
| The map throws `Unable to load glyph range …` errors | `glyphs` URL points at a server that lacks the requested range, or the font-stack name is miss-spelled. | Verify the folder names on the server match the `text-font` strings exactly. |
| Labels flicker on first load, then look correct | Plugin loaded **asynchronously** but the map rendered a frame before it was ready. | Pass `true` as the third argument to `setRTLTextPlugin` (synchronous load) **or** call `map.once('load', …)` after the plugin’s callback. |

---

## 6. Full minimal example (GL JS 6.8 – needs plugin)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>Arabic & Hebrew RTL labels</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@6.8.2/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@6.8.2/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <!-- 1️⃣ RTL plugin -->
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/@mapbox/mapbox-gl-rtl-text@0.3.0/dist/mapbox-gl-rtl-text.js"></script>
  <script>
    // 2️⃣ Register the plugin *before* creating the map
    maplibregl.setRTLTextPlugin(
      'https://unpkg.com/@mapbox/mapbox-gl-rtl-text@0.3.0/dist/mapbox-gl-rtl-text.js',
      function (err) {
        if (err) console.error('RTL plugin error', err);
        else console.log('RTL plugin ready');
      },
      false // async
    );
  </script>
  <style>
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      "version": 8,
      "glyphs": "https://demotiles.maplibre.org/font/{fontstack}/{range}.pbf",
      "sources": {
        "osm": {
          "type": "vector",
          "url": "https://demotiles.maplibre.org/tiles/planet.json"
        }
      },
      "layers": [
        {
          "id": "place-labels",
          "type": "symbol",
          "source": "osm",
          "source-layer": "place",
          "layout": {
            "text-field": ["get", "name"],
            "text-font": [
              "Noto Sans Arabic Regular",
              "Noto Sans Hebrew Regular",
              "Noto Sans Regular"
            ],
            "text-size": 14,
            "text-anchor": "center"
          },
          "paint": {
            "text-color": "#222"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    center: [35, 31], // somewhere in the Middle East
    zoom: 5
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

*If you upgrade to **MapLibre GL JS 6.9.0** or newer, simply delete the `<script>` that loads the RTL plugin and the `setRTLTextPlugin` call

Below is a step-by-step recipe you can copy-paste into a MapLibre GL JS app to turn a **raster-DEM** tile set into a hillshade layer.

---

## 1. Make sure you have a raster-DEM source

A hillshade layer can only read from a source whose `type` is **`raster-dem`**.  
Typical public sources:

| Provider | URL template | Notes |
|----------|--------------|-------|
| **MapLibre demo tiles** | `https://demotiles.maplibre.org/terrain-tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.png` | 256 px tiles, EPSG:3857, 0-255 elevation encoded as grayscale |
| **OpenMapTiles terrain** | `https://tiles.openmaptiles.org/terrain/{z}/{x}/{y}.png` | Same format, hosted by OMT |
| **Your own GeoTIFF → MBTiles → raster-DEM** | `https://mydomain.com/tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.png` | Must be pre-processed to 256 px PNG/WEBP with elevation in the red channel (or use MapLibre’s `raster-dem` encoding) |

> **Tip:** If you already have a `terrain` source for 3-D terrain (`type: "raster-dem"`), you can reuse it for hillshade – you just need a separate layer that points at the same source.

---

## 2. Add the source to the style

```js
// Assuming you already have a MapLibre map instance called `map`
map.addSource('my-dem', {
  type: 'raster-dem',
  // The URL must end with `{z}/{x}/{y}` (or `{z}/{x}/{y}@2x` for high-dpi)
  url: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/terrain-tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
  tileSize: 256,          // most DEM tiles are 256 px
  // optional – helps the browser cache the tiles
  maxzoom: 14,
  // optional – tell MapLibre the data are elevation values (0-255)
  // (the default is "mapbox" which expects 0-255 grayscale)
  // encoding: 'mapbox'   // keep default unless you used a different format
});
```

> **CORS** – The tile server must send `Access-Control-Allow-Origin:*` (or your domain) so the browser can fetch the tiles.

---

## 3. Add the hillshade layer

```js
map.addLayer({
  id: 'hillshade',
  type: 'hillshade',
  source: 'my-dem',          // matches the source id above
  // optional: you can limit the layer to a specific zoom range
  minzoom: 0,
  maxzoom: 14,

  // Paint properties – tweak to taste
  paint: {
    // Light direction (azimuth in degrees, 0 = north, clockwise)
    'hillshade-illumination-direction': 315,
    // Light altitude (degrees above the horizon)
    'hillshade-illumination-anchor': 'map', // or 'viewport'
    'hillshade-exaggeration': 0.5,          // 0-1, higher = more contrast
    // Colors for the three shading components
    'hillshade-shadow-color': '#000000',
    'hillshade-highlight-color': '#ffffff',
    'hillshade-accent-color': '#777777'
  }
});
```

### What the paint properties do

| Property | Typical range | Effect |
|----------|---------------|--------|
| `hillshade-illumination-direction` | 0-360° | Sun azimuth; rotate to change where shadows fall |
| `hillshade-illumination-anchor` | `'map'` or `'viewport'` | Keep the sun fixed to the map (rotates with map) or to the screen |
| `hillshade-exaggeration` | 0-1 (default 0.5) | Boosts contrast; set to `1` for maximum depth |
| `hillshade-shadow-color` / `highlight-color` / `accent-color` | Any CSS color | Tweak the visual style (e.g., dark-mode palettes) |

---

## 4. Order the layer correctly

Hillshade is a **background** visual effect, so you usually want it **below** most vector layers (roads, labels, etc.) but **above** any raster base-maps you might have.

```js
// Example: insert just before the first label layer
const firstLabel = map.getStyle().layers.find(l => l.type === 'symbol' && l.layout['text-field']);
map.moveLayer('hillshade', firstLabel ? firstLabel.id : undefined);
```

If you have a raster basemap (e.g., satellite imagery) you may want the hillshade **on top** of that raster:

```js
map.moveLayer('hillshade', 'satellite-basemap'); // place hillshade right after the raster basemap
```

---

## 5. (Optional) Combine with 3-D terrain

If you also want a 3-D terrain surface, add a `terrain` property to the style:

```js
map.setTerrain({ source: 'my-dem', exaggeration: 1.5 });
```

The hillshade layer will then shade the 3-D terrain automatically – no extra work needed.

---

## 6. Full minimal example

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre Hillshade Demo</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {
        // a simple OSM vector basemap (optional)
        'osm': {
          type: 'vector',
          url: 'https://basemaps.cartocdn.com/rastertiles/voyager/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
          // you could also use any other vector source here
        }
      },
      layers: [
        {
          id: 'osm-basemap',
          type: 'raster',
          source: 'osm'
        }
      ]
    },
    center: [0, 0],
    zoom: 2
  });

  map.on('load', () => {
    // 1️⃣ DEM source
    map.addSource('my-dem', {
      type: 'raster-dem',
      url: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/terrain-tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
      tileSize: 256,
      maxzoom: 14
    });

    // 2️⃣ Hillshade layer
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'hillshade',
      type: 'hillshade',
      source: 'my-dem',
      paint: {
        'hillshade-illumination-direction': 315,
        'hillshade-exaggeration': 0.6,
        'hillshade-shadow-color': '#000000',
        'hillshade-highlight-color': '#ffffff',
        'hillshade-accent-color': '#777777'
      }
    });

    // (optional) 3️⃣ 3-D terrain
    // map.setTerrain({ source: 'my-dem', exaggeration: 1.5 });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Open the page in a browser and you’ll see a shaded relief map. Adjust the paint values or the illumination direction to match your design.

---

## 7. Common pitfalls & troubleshooting

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| **No hillshade appears** (just a blank or plain background) | DEM source not loading (CORS, wrong URL, wrong tile size) | Open the tile URL directly in the browser; check network console for 404 or CORS errors. |
| **Only a faint gray layer** | `hillshade-exaggeration` is too low or the DEM elevation range is narrow (e.g., a flat ocean tile set) | Increase `hillshade-exaggeration` (0.5 → 0.8) or use a DEM with more relief. |
| **Shadows point the wrong way** | Wrong `hillshade-illumination-direction` or you’re using `hillshade-illumination-anchor: 'viewport'` while rotating the map | Set `hillshade-illumination-anchor: 'map'` to keep the sun fixed to north-south, or adjust the direction value. |
| **Labels disappear after adding hillshade** | Hillshade layer placed **above** the label (`symbol`) layers | Move the hillshade layer below the first label layer (`map.moveLayer('hillshade', firstLabel.id)`). |
| **Hillshade looks blocky at high zoom** | DEM tiles are low resolution (e.g., maxzoom 8) and you’re zooming beyond that | Use a higher-resolution DEM source or increase `maxzoom` on the source if the tiles exist. |
| **CORS error on custom DEM tiles** | Server does not send `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` header | Add the header on the server (e.g., `add_header Access-Control-Allow-Origin *;` for Nginx) or proxy the tiles through a CORS-enabled endpoint. |

---

## 8. References

* **MapLibre GL JS style spec – `raster-dem` source**: <https://maplibre.org/maplibre-style-spec/sources/#raster-dem>
* **Hillshade layer type**: <https://maplibre.org/maplibre-style-spec/layers/#hillshade>
* **Terrain & hillshade demo** (official): <https://maplibre.org/maplibre-gl-js/docs/examples/terrain/>
* **OpenMapTiles terrain tiles**: <https://openmaptiles.org/terrain/>

---

### TL;DR

1. **Add a `raster-dem` source** pointing at your DEM tiles.  
2. **Add a `hillshade` layer** that uses that source and set the paint properties you like.  
3. **Place the layer** below labels (or above a raster basemap) and you’re done.

Happy shading!

Below is a **step-by-step recipe** you can copy-paste into a MapLibre GL JS app (or into a style JSON) to turn a raster-DEM tile set into a hillshade layer.

---

## 1. Make sure you have a **raster-DEM source**

The source must be of type **`raster-dem`** (not `raster`).  
Typical providers:

| Provider | URL template | Notes |
|----------|--------------|-------|
| **MapLibre demo tiles** | `https://demotiles.maplibre.org/terrain-tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.png` | Small, public DEM (256 px tiles, elevation encoded as 16-bit PNG) |
| **OpenMapTiles terrain** | `https://tiles.openmaptiles.org/terrain/{z}/{x}/{y}.png` | Same format, but you need an API key for the hosted service |
| **Your own MBTiles / Cloud-Optimized GeoTIFF** | `https://myserver.com/tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.png` | Must be served with CORS headers (`Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *`) |

> **Important:** The DEM tiles must be **encoded as 16-bit PNG** (or `webp`/`jpeg` with the MapLibre “terrain-encoding” extension). If you have a GeoTIFF you can convert it with `gdal2tiles.py` – `-r near -t_srs EPSG:3857 -a_srs EPSG:4326 -z 0-14 -f png -p raster -s EPSG:3857`.

### Adding the source in JavaScript

```js
map.addSource('my-dem', {
  type: 'raster-dem',
  // The URL can contain {z}/{x}/{y} placeholders
  url: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/terrain-tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
  tileSize: 256,          // 256 px is the default; 512 works too
  // optional – helps the browser cache correctly
  maxzoom: 14,
  // optional – limits the area you request (helps with CORS & performance)
  bounds: [-180, -85, 180, 85],
  // tells MapLibre that the PNG encodes elevation in 16-bit signed integers
  // (the demo tiles use the Mapbox “mapbox://tileset/v1/.../terrain” encoding)
  encoding: 'mapbox'      // or 'terrarium' if you use that scheme
});
```

> **Native tip:** In MapLibre Native the same source definition works; just make sure the `encoding` matches the tile set you serve.

---

## 2. Add a **hillshade layer** that points at that source

```js
map.addLayer({
  id: 'hillshade',
  type: 'hillshade',
  source: 'my-dem',
  // The raster-DEM source can be used directly; no need for a separate raster layer.
  paint: {
    // Optional – tweak the visual style
    'hillshade-illumination-direction': 315, // sun direction (degrees clockwise from north)
    'hillshade-exaggeration': 0.5,           // vertical exaggeration (1 = true scale)
    'hillshade-shadow-color': 'rgba(0,0,0,0.5)',
    'hillshade-highlight-color': 'rgba(255,255,255,0.5)'
  }
});
```

### What the paint properties do

| Property | Typical range | Effect |
|----------|---------------|--------|
| `hillshade-illumination-direction` | 0-360° | Sun azimuth; 315° (north-west) is the classic “shaded relief” look. |
| `hillshade-exaggeration` | 0-2 (or more) | Scales the height of the terrain; >1 makes mountains look steeper. |
| `hillshade-shadow-color` | any CSS color | Darkening of slopes facing away from the sun. |
| `hillshade-highlight-color` | any CSS color | Lightening of slopes facing the sun. |
| `hillshade-accent-color` | any CSS color | Optional extra contrast on the steepest parts. |

You can omit the `paint` block entirely – the defaults give a decent gray-scale hillshade.

---

## 3. (Optional) Insert the layer at a specific position

If you already have other layers (e.g., a base raster or vector tiles), you may want the hillshade **under** them:

```js
// Insert just above the first symbol layer (labels) but below any raster layers
const firstSymbol = map.getStyle().layers.find(l => l.type === 'symbol');
map.addLayer(hillshadeLayer, firstSymbol.id);
```

Or, if you prefer to keep it at the bottom:

```js
map.moveLayer('hillshade', map.getStyle().layers[0].id); // moves it to the top of the stack
```

---

## 4. Full minimal example

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre hillshade demo</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>#map {position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%;}</style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {
        // a simple OSM vector base (optional)
        'osm': {
          type: 'vector',
          url: 'https://basemaps.cartocdn.com/rastertiles/voyager/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
          // you could also use a vector source here
        }
      },
      layers: [
        {
          id: 'osm-base',
          type: 'raster',
          source: 'osm'
        }
      ]
    },
    center: [0, 0],
    zoom: 2,
    // optional: improve CJK rendering, etc.
    // localIdeographFontFamily: '"Noto Sans CJK SC", sans-serif'
  });

  map.on('load', () => {
    // 1️⃣ DEM source
    map.addSource('my-dem', {
      type: 'raster-dem',
      url: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/terrain-tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
      tileSize: 256,
      encoding: 'mapbox'
    });

    // 2️⃣ Hillshade layer
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'hillshade',
      type: 'hillshade',
      source: 'my-dem',
      paint: {
        'hillshade-illumination-direction': 315,
        'hillshade-exaggeration': 0.7
      }
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Open the page in a browser and you’ll see a gray-scale relief draped over the base map.

---

## 5. Common pitfalls & how to fix them

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| **No hillshade appears (just the base map)** | DEM source not loading (404, CORS, wrong URL) | Open the tile URL directly in the browser → you should see a PNG. Check network tab for 404 or CORS errors. Add `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` on the tile server. |
| **Hillshade looks flat / no contrast** | `encoding` mismatch (default is `mapbox`, but your tiles use `terrarium`) | Set `encoding: 'terrarium'` (or `'mapbox'` if you’re using Mapbox-style tiles). |
| **Very noisy or blocky hillshade** | Using 512-px tiles with a 256-px `tileSize` (or vice-versa) | Make `tileSize` match the actual tile size, or regenerate tiles at the size you declare. |
| **Labels disappear after adding hillshade** | Hillshade layer placed **above** label layers | Insert the hillshade **below** the label layer (`addLayer(..., beforeId)`). |
| **Performance drops on mobile** | Very high `maxzoom` (e.g., 22) for a large DEM | Limit `maxzoom` to the highest zoom you actually need (e.g., 14-15). |
| **Hillshade looks wrong on the edges of the map** | DEM tiles don’t cover the whole viewport (bounds missing) | Provide a `bounds` array that matches the tile set, or use a larger DEM source. |

---

## 6. Using a **style JSON** instead of JS

If you prefer to keep everything in a static style file, add the source and layer like this:

```json
{
  "version": 8,
  "sources": {
    "my-dem": {
      "type": "raster-dem",
      "url": "https://demotiles.maplibre.org/terrain-tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.png",
      "tileSize": 256,
      "encoding": "mapbox"
    },
    "osm": {
      "type": "raster",
      "url": "https://basemaps.cartocdn.com/rastertiles/voyager/{z}/{x}/{y}.png",
      "tileSize": 256
    }
  },
  "layers": [
    { "id": "osm-base", "type": "raster", "source": "osm" },
    {
      "id": "hillshade",
      "type": "hillshade",
      "source": "my-dem",
      "paint": {
        "hillshade-illumination-direction": 315,
        "hillshade-exaggeration": 0.6
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

Load it with:

```js
new maplibregl.Map({ container: 'map', style: 'path/to/your-style.json' });
```

---

## 7. Quick checklist before you ship

1. **DEM source reachable** – test a tile URL in the browser.  
2. **CORS headers** – `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` (or your domain).  
3. **Encoding matches** – `mapbox` vs `terrarium`.  
4. **Layer order** – hillshade below labels, above base raster.  
5. **Performance** – limit `maxzoom`, use 256 px tiles, enable HTTP/2 or CDN.  
6. **Mobile testing** – verify the hillshade renders smoothly on low-end devices.

---

### TL;DR

```js
map.addSource('my-dem', {
  type: 'raster-dem',
  url: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/terrain-tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
  tileSize: 256,
  encoding: 'mapbox'
});

map.addLayer({
  id: 'hillshade',
  type: 'hillshade',
  source: 'my-dem',
  paint: {
    'hillshade-illumination-direction': 315,
    'hillshade-exaggeration': 0.5
  }
});
```

That’s all you need to get a hillshade layer working with a raster-DEM source in MapLibre GL JS. Happy mapping!
